WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:08.380
Alkenes are useful synthetic intermediates;
their double bonds can undergo a variety of

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:13.920
chemical transformations, providing useful
functional groups like halides, alcohols,

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.000
and carbonyl compounds.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.580
Let’s review some of the major reactions
that alkenes undergo.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:26.850
An alkene can be reacted with hydrogen gas
in the presence of a metal catalyst to produce

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:28.520
alkanes.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:36.190
The hydrogen gas adds to one face of the alkene,
so we get syn addition of H2 across the double

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:37.629
bond.

00:00:37.629 --> 00:00:43.749
Alkenes react with halogen-containing acids,
like hydrochloric and hydrobromic acid, adding

00:00:43.749 --> 00:00:47.730
a single halogen across the double bond.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:52.969
If the alkene is reacted with acid in the
presence of water or an alcohol, the result

00:00:52.969 --> 00:00:56.140
is an alcohol or an ether.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:00.960
Water or the alcohol can be used as solvent
in this reaction.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:06.350
You may have noticed these reactions are regioselective;
in our example, the new functional group is

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:11.650
added to the central carbon, and not the terminal
carbon of the alkene.

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:16.470
This is because these reactions form a carbocation
intermediate.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:22.660
The more substituted carbocation forms because
it is more stable.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:28.210
Because carbocations are trigonal planar in
geometry, they can be attacked by the nucleophile

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:33.770
from the top face or the bottom face, so if
there is a stereocenter produced in the reaction,

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:40.170
it will be a mixture of R and S. When this
happens, we say a “racemic mixture” is

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:41.460
produced.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:46.460
The addition of the nucleophile to the more
substituted carbon of the alkene is sometimes

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:53.630
referred to as Markovnikov addition and is
related to carbocation stability.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:02:00.330
Rearrangements via hydride or alkyl shift
are possible with these acid-promoted reactions.

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:05.470
Treatment of an alkene with liquid bromine
or chlorine gas adds two atoms of the halogen

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:12.260
across the double bond, and if the reaction
is carried out in water, a halohydrin is formed.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:17.760
The latter reaction is again regioselective
and stereoselective.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:25.040
A bromonium ion forms, and the nucleophile
attacks at the more substituted carbon atom,

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:30.719
because there is significant contribution
of the more stable carbocation resonance form

00:02:30.719 --> 00:02:32.109
here.

00:02:32.109 --> 00:02:35.080
This accounts for the regioselectivity.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:40.430
The functional groups are also added to the
molecule stereoselectively.

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:45.230
Because the bromonium ion blocks one face
of the molecule, the nucleophile must approach

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:47.249
from the opposite side.

00:02:47.249 --> 00:02:49.040
We call this anti addition.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:55.159
Allylic bromination is a radical reaction
that adds a bromine atom to the carbon adjacent

00:02:55.159 --> 00:03:00.640
to the double bond, while preserving the alkene
functional group.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:06.989
Oxymercuration also forms alcohols from alkenes
in a Markovnikov fashion.

00:03:06.989 --> 00:03:12.549
A mercurinium ion is formed, which reacts
with water at the more substituted carbon

00:03:12.549 --> 00:03:14.310
atom.

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:20.829
Treatment with sodium borohydride reduces
the mercury, removing it from the molecule.

00:03:20.829 --> 00:03:26.849
The major advantage of this reaction is that
carbocation rearrangements by hydride or alkyl

00:03:26.849 --> 00:03:33.609
shift are not possible, although mercury is
a rather toxic element to use in the lab.

00:03:33.609 --> 00:03:41.049
Hydroboration/oxidation forms the less substituted
alcohol via non-Markovnikov addition.

00:03:41.049 --> 00:03:47.519
Boron is often substituted with large R groups
to make this reaction more selective; examples

00:03:47.519 --> 00:03:54.870
of commonly used reagents are 9-BBN, disiamyl
borane, and borane-THF.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:04:00.999
The large boron group adds to the least sterically
hindered side of the alkene, while the small

00:04:00.999 --> 00:04:06.590
hydride atom is added to the more substituted
carbon atom.

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:11.799
Treatment of the boron complex with sodium
hydroxide and peroxide in a second stage of

00:04:11.799 --> 00:04:16.010
the reaction produces the alcohol.

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:21.019
The addition of two hydroxyl groups across
the alkene is accomplished by treatment with

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:25.879
osmium tetraoxide, which forms an osmate ester.

00:04:25.879 --> 00:04:31.560
This reaction gives a syn diol because osmium
approaches from one face.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:37.030
One molar equivalent of a reducing agent is
required to cleave osmium from the molecule

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:40.330
and produce the diol.

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:45.639
Ozonolysis essentially splits a double bond
in half, creating a carbonyl compound from

00:04:45.639 --> 00:04:47.509
each side.

00:04:47.509 --> 00:04:52.910
In our example, a substituted aldehyde and
formaldehyde are formed.

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:57.620
We need to take a look at the mechanism to
understand what is going on here.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:03.500
The addition of ozone forms a molozonide,
which rearranges to form the ozonide.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:06.940
In a second step, dimethyl sulfate is added.

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:13.460
This cleaves the oxygen-oxygen bond, and the
charged intermediate collapses to form carbonyl

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:17.180
compounds and dimethylsulfoxide.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:23.069
The Wittig reaction is an alkene forming reaction
that seems relevant to touch on here.

00:05:23.069 --> 00:05:31.060
A carbonyl compound is converted into an alkene
by treatment with an organophosphorous reagent.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:36.349
This “Wittig reagent” is a phosphorous
ylide; although we can draw a resonance structure

00:05:36.349 --> 00:05:43.000
with a double bond, there is also contribution
from a neutral dipolar form.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:48.360
The carbanion form makes it a bit more apparent
how the carbon atom of the Wittig reagent

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:53.919
ends up bonded to the electrophilic carbonyl
carbon of an aldehyde or ketone.

00:05:53.919 --> 00:05:58.199
The side product of this reaction is triphenylphosphene
oxide.

00:05:58.199 --> 00:06:04.680
Z-Alkenes are favored under classical Wittig
conditions; when there is a possibility for

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:11.530
resonance stabilization of the carbanion,
the E-alkene is more favored.

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:17.830
Alkene metathesis, though a bit more advanced,
is an important reaction in modern synthesis.

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:23.639
Treatment of two alkenes with molybdenum or
ruthenium catalysts results in the formation

00:06:23.639 --> 00:06:30.319
of a new alkene with substitution from the
original alkenes on either side of the double

00:06:30.319 --> 00:06:31.319
bond.

00:06:31.319 --> 00:06:37.190
This reaction is most useful when it done
in an intramolecular fashion to close a ring.

